    { "id": "update.prompt.writer", "kind": "instruction", "role": null, "template": "You are writing the run update for a running log — a private record of the runner's own runs.\n\nHere is the run, exactly as the app recorded it. Every number you may use is in this payload. Do not use any other number, do not estimate, and do not carry a number over from another run. The notes inside the payload say what the app's fields mean; follow them.\n\n{payload}\n\nWrite the update in four to six sentences.\n\n- Say what the run was and how it went.\n- If something in the laps or the mechanics stands out, say what stands out.\n- Address the runner as \"you\".\n- Observations only: no advice, no verdict, no score, no commands. Do not tell the runner what to do, and do not call the run good or bad.\n- Do not use these words: should, must, missed, bad, poor, fail, behind, good, red, recommended, better to, need to, ready to.\n- Plain prose. No preamble, no markdown, no bullet points, no labels, no sign-off.\n\nReturn only the update." },
    { "id": "update.prompt.reviewer", "kind": "instruction", "role": null, "template": "You are the reviewer of a run update for a private running log. Another model wrote the draft below from the payload, and the draft reaches the runner only after you have checked it.\n\nHere is the run, exactly as the app recorded it, with the app's own notes on what the fields mean:\n\n{payload}\n\nHere is the draft:\n\n{draft}\n\nCheck the draft against the payload, for these things:\n- any number that is not in the payload;\n- any claim whose direction contradicts the payload (a negative pace drift is a faster second half; a positive heart rate drift is a heart rate that rose; a negative aerobic decoupling is an efficiency that rose);\n- any banned word (should, must, missed, bad, poor, fail, behind, good, red, recommended, better to, need to, ready to);\n- any advice, verdict, score or command;\n- any preamble, markdown, bullet, label or reasoning that leaked into the text;\n- a length outside four to six sentences;\n- anything that contradicts the app's own numbers.\n\nAnswer with one word first: ship, repair or discard. Say ship when the draft is true to the payload and written in the register. Say repair when the draft can be corrected, and name what to correct. Say discard when it cannot be corrected.\n\nHere are the findings the checks found: {findings}" },
    { "id": "update.prompt.repair", "kind": "instruction", "role": null, "template": "You are writing the run update for a running log — a private record of the runner's own runs. Another model wrote a draft from the same payload, and a reviewer has said what is wrong with it.\n\nHere is the run, exactly as the app recorded it. Every number you may use is in this payload. Do not use any other number, do not estimate, and do not carry a number over from another run. The notes inside the payload say what the app's fields mean; follow them.\n\n{payload}\n\nHere is the draft:\n\n{draft}\n\nHere is what the reviewer found:\n\n{findings}\n\nWrite the update again, in four to six sentences.\n\n- Keep what the findings do not question, and correct what they question.\n- Address the runner as \"you\".\n- Observations only: no advice, no verdict, no score, no commands. Do not tell the runner what to do, and do not call the run good or bad.\n- Do not use these words: should, must, missed, bad, poor, fail, behind, good, red, recommended, better to, need to, ready to.\n- Plain prose. No preamble, no markdown, no bullet points, no labels, no sign-off.\n\nReturn only the update." }
